    {
      "slug": "kuruman-marketplace",
      "title": "Kuruman MarketPlace",
      "year": "2026",
      "kind": "Advanced Diploma project",
      "isLive": true,
      "layout": "media",
      "summary": "A marketplace that brings Kuruman's small and informal businesses online: market stalls, food sellers, tailors, salons and local couriers. It is designed for real conditions, including basic phones, limited data and three languages, and is covered by 16 integration test suites and 37 Python unit tests.",
      "stack": [ "React", "TypeScript", "Node.js", "Express", "PostgreSQL", "Python", "scikit-learn", "Render" ],
      "links": [
        { "label": "Live site", "url": "https://kurumanmarketplace.co.za" },
        { "label": "Source code", "url": "https://github.com/PhemeloMartin/Kuruman_MarketPlace" }
      ],
      "highlights": [
        { "title": "SMS ordering.", "text": "Market stalls accept or decline orders from a basic phone by replying to a text." },
        { "title": "Consistent data.", "text": "PostgreSQL row locking keeps stock and payments correct when two people buy at once." },
        { "title": "Security.", "text": "Payments are verified server-side, staff use two-factor sign-in, and ID documents are encrypted at rest." },
        { "title": "Machine learning.", "text": "Four scikit-learn models suggest categories, analyse reviews, flag scams and forecast sales." }
      ],
      "image": {
        "src": "/img/kuruman-marketplace.jpg",
        "alt": "Kuruman MarketPlace home page: a search box over a photo of a Kuruman street market, with options to buy or sell.",
        "width": 1440,
        "height": 900,
        "browserUrl": "kurumanmarketplace.co.za"
      },
      "caseStudy": [
        {
          "heading": "The problem",
          "paragraphs": [
            "Most of Kuruman's small businesses have no online presence. They sell to whoever walks past, take orders in scattered WhatsApp chats and handle cash with no record. Customers can't see what's for sale without travelling to town, and there is no trusted way to pay, deliver or sort out a problem.",
            "Many residents use basic phones or have little data, and many prefer Setswana or Afrikaans, so the solution had to work under those conditions rather than assume a modern smartphone."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "What I built",
          "paragraphs": [ "A mobile-first Progressive Web App with a separate experience for each kind of user." ],
          "bullets": [
            "Customers browse markets, search in English, Setswana or Afrikaans, pay cash or online with Payfast, and track their courier live on a map.",
            "Sellers get a shop page with photos and voice notes, order management, sales KPIs and a 7-day sales forecast.",
            "Market stalls take orders by SMS from a basic phone: reply 1 to accept, 2 if sold out, or the customer's code to hand over.",
            "Couriers claim delivery jobs, get directions inside the app, and hand over orders with a one-time code or QR code.",
            "Support staff work in a console behind two-factor sign-in, handling seller and courier verification, refunds and the audit log."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Architecture",
          "paragraphs": [
            "One Node.js and Express service on Render serves both the React app and the REST API from a single address, so the login cookie and the CSRF origin check work without cross-site cookies. Sessions live in PostgreSQL, which keeps the API stateless and ready to scale out.",
            "Background jobs handle order expiry, an outbox for SMS and notifications, the AI worker and data retention. The machine-learning models run in a separate Python service because they need a different runtime."
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Integrations: Payfast (payments), BulkSMS (SMS), Twilio (calls), Resend (email), Cloudinary (photos) and OSRM routing on OpenStreetMap.",
            "Hosting: Render, defined as code in a Blueprint file and deployed automatically on every push to main."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Database design",
          "paragraphs": [ "PostgreSQL on Neon with 33 tables, chosen because the data is relational and money and stock need ACID transactions." ],
          "bullets": [
            "SELECT … FOR UPDATE row locks stop two customers buying the last item and stop a payment being applied twice.",
            "The database enforces its own rules: check constraints on stock and ratings, prices stored as integer cents, and partial unique indexes such as one applied payment per order.",
            "Order lines keep a price snapshot, so a later price change never alters a past order.",
            "The audit log is append-only, enforced by a database trigger."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Security and privacy",
          "bullets": [
            "Sign-in by SMS code: 6 digits, valid for 10 minutes, stored only as a hash and rate-limited.",
            "Server-side sessions in httpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies; the database keeps only a SHA-256 hash of each token.",
            "Support staff use a passphrase plus an authenticator app (TOTP) with replay protection.",
            "ID documents and authenticator secrets are encrypted at rest with AES-256-GCM.",
            "An order is marked paid only after Payfast's server notification passes signature, source IP, amount and merchant checks.",
            "Personal data is deleted on a schedule in line with POPIA, and verification documents are never emailed."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Machine learning",
          "paragraphs": [ "Four models trained with scikit-learn. They advise and people decide: nothing is blocked, hidden or priced automatically." ],
          "bullets": [
            "Category suggestions in three languages, which abstain when unsure.",
            "Review analysis for sentiment and recurring themes.",
            "Scam screening that flags attempts to move payment off the site for support staff to review.",
            "A 7-day sales forecast per product using Poisson regression with weekday and payday effects.",
            "Each model is loaded only if its SHA-256 hash matches its model card."
          ]
        },
        {
          "heading": "Testing",
          "paragraphs": [ "16 automated integration suites with 204 checks cover payments, refunds, sign-in, privacy requests, SMS ordering and courier tracking. The Python AI component has 37 unit tests." ]
        }
      ]
    },
